          %h2#introduction Introduction
          %h3#Overview Overview
          %p
            %em Globular
            is a web-based
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
            for finitely-presented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semistrict"} semistrict
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+set"} globular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+categories"} higher categories
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+n-category"}>
              associative
              = succeed "-categories" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            ”). It allows one to formalize higher-categorical proofs in finitely-presented
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-categories"} n-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science"} http://globular.science
          %p
            %strong We recommend the Chrome browser.
          %p
            %i Globular
            currently operates up to the level of 4-categories. By the yoga of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/k-tuply+monoidal+n-category"} k-tuply monoidal n-categories
          %ul
            %li Monoidal 3-category
            %li Monoidal 2-category, optionally with a braiding
            %li Monoidal category, optionally with a braiding or symmetry
          %p
            The model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semistrict"} semistrict
            higher categories that
            %em Globular
            uses puts all the weak structure in the interchangers, and higher associated structures. At the level of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-categories"} 3-categories
            %em Globular
            implements the axioms of a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+category"} Gray category
          %p
            %em Globular
            is free to use, and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/jamievicary/globular"} open-source
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/jamie.vicary/"} Jamie Vicary
            %em Globular
            has been developed in the
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/activities/quantum/"} Quantum Group
            of the
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/"} Department of Computer Science
            at the
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.ox.ac.uk/"} University of Oxford
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/krzysztof.bar/"} Krzysztof Bar
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ru.nl/A.Kissinger/"} Aleks Kissinger
            %a{:href => "http://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/jamie.vicary/"} Jamie Vicary
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => ""} Caspar Wylie
          %p
            If you’ve never used
            %i Globular
            before, the
            %a{:href => "https://youtu.be/QXZJvXgs0xk"} tutorial video
            is a good place to start. To report a bug, please use the
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/jamievicary/globular/issues"} issue tracker
          %p We are extremely grateful to John Baez, Manuel Bärenz, Bruce Bartlett, Eugenia Cheng, Chris Douglas, Eric Finster, Nick Gurski, André Henriques, Samuel Mimram and Dominic Verdon for useful discussions.
          %p#HowToCite
            If you would like to cite
            = succeed "," do
              %em Globular
          %pre
            %code
              :preserve
                  @InProceedings{
                  globular, 
                  author={Krzysztof Bar and Aleks Kissinger and Jamie Vicary}, 
                  title={Globular: an online proof assistant for higher-dimensional rewriting}, 
                  note={\href{http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Globular}{ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Globular}}, 
                  pages={34:1--34:11}, 
                  volume=52, 
                  year=2016, 
                  booktitle={Leibniz International Proceedings in Informatics}}          %h3#example_proofs Example proofs
          %p
            To see what can be done with
            = succeed "," do
              %em Globular
            %em Project
            and
            %em Slice
            controls in the top-right of the main diagram (see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "#changingtheview"} Changing the view
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Frobenius implies associative
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.004"}> globular.science/1512.004
              ). In a monoidal category, if multiplication and comultiplication morphisms are unital, counital and Frobenius, then they are associative and coassociative.
            %li
              %strong Strengthening an equivalence
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.007"}> globular.science/1512.007
              ). In a 2-category, an equivalence gives rise to an adjoint equivalence, satisfying the snake equations.
            %li
              %strong
                Pentagon and triangle implies
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mi I
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_I = \rho_I
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.002"}> globular.science/1512.002
              ). In a monoidal 2-category, if a pseudomonoid object satisfies pentagon and triangle equations, then it satisfies
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mi I
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi ρ
                        %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda_I = \rho_I
            %li
              %strong The antipode is an algebra homomorphism
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.011"}> globular.science/1512.011
              ). For a Hopf algebra structure in a braided monoidal category, the antipode is an algebra anti-homomorphism. (Thanks to
              %a{:href => "https://www.cs.ox.ac.uk/people/dominic.verdon/"} Dominic Verdon
              for formalizing this.)
            %li
              %strong The Perko knots are isotopic
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.012"}> globular.science/1512.012
              ). The
              %a{:href => "http://mathforum.org/mathimages/index.php/Perko_pair_knots"} Perko knots
              are a pair of 10-crossing knots stated by Little in 1899 to be distinct, but proven by Perko in 1974 to be isotopic. Here we give the isotopy proof. A nice feature is that the second and third Reidemeister moves do not have to be entered, since they are already implied by the 3-category axioms.
            %li
              %strong The pants bordism is commutative
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1601.005"}> globular.science/1601.005
              ). For an oriented dualizable object in a monoidal 2-category, the associated pants bordism is commutative. (Based on unpublished notes by
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.southalabama.edu/mathstat/personal_pages/carter/"} Scott Carter
            %li
              %strong K=P#P
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1605.003"}> globular.science/1605.003
              ). As knotted spheres in 4 dimensions, the Klein bottle is isotopic to the connect sum of two cross caps. (Based on the movie move proof in Section 2.3 of
              %a{:href => "https://books.google.ca/books/about/Knotted_Surfaces_and_Their_Diagrams.html?id=zN7xBwAAQBAJ&redir_esc=y"}
                %em Knotted Surfaces and their Diagrams
              by Scott Carter and Masahico Saito.)
            %li
              %strong The 1-twist spun trefoil is unknotted.
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1607.003"}> globular.science/1607.003
              ). For every classical (1,1)-knot, the knotted surface in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
              given by its 1-twist spin is unknotted. Here we give the explicit unknotting isotopy for the trefoil. (Based on the movie move proof in Carter and Saito. Formalization by Scott Carter.)
            %li
              %strong Swallowtail coherence
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1512.006"}> globular.science/1512.006
              ). In a 3-category, an adjunction of 1-morphisms gives rise to a coherent adjunction satisfying the swallowtail equations.
            %li
              %strong Butterfly coherence
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1605.002"}> globular.science/1605.002
              ). In a 4-category, an adjunction of 1-morphisms gives rise to a coherent adjunction satisfying the butterfly equations. (Developed by Krzysztof Bar and Jamie Vicary.)
            %li
              %strong Kirby calculus and 4-manifolds
              (
              %a{:href => "http://globular.science/1808.001"}> globular.science/1808.001
              ). It’s well-known that the braid groups are 3-endomorphism spaces in the free 3-category over a single object and a single (nontrivial) 2-morphism. In Kirby calculus (using the “dotted circle convention” by Akbulut), smooth 4-manifolds are specified by links. By adding a few more generators and relations (for handle slides and handle cancellations, a.k.a. Kirby moves), Kirby diagrams are the endomorphisms of the 2-identity in the previously mentioned 3-category. Some example manifolds are defined and some example diffeomorphisms are formalised. (By Manuel Bärenz)
          %h2#basic_functionality Basic functionality
          %h3#overview_2 Overview
          %p
            %strong Signatures and diagrams.
            Using
            %em Globular
            involves two main techniques: building the
            = succeed "," do
              %em signature
            %em diagram
            over the signature. The signature is the list of cells that are available, and the diagram is a particular composite of those cells. A diagram can be thought of as a composite cell in the free semistrict
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            The signature can be enlarged by building diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/globular+set"} globularity conditions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(S) = s(T)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(S) = t(T)
          %p
            Diagrams are built by clicking on cells of the signature, and by clicking and dragging on the diagram itself. Signature cells of dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n+1
            act on
            = succeed "-diagrams" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %em rewriting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            and replacing it with a subdiagram of shape
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %p
            The only things that Globular understands are
            = succeed "-cells," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f=g
            holds between
            = succeed "-cells," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo :
                  %mi f
                  %mo →
                  %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:f \to g
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi g
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:g \to f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[f]
            involving
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            to obtain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[g]
            %em equation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo =
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C[f]=C[g]
            = succeed "." do
              %em equality via rewriting
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            %strong Accounts.
            If you create an account on the site, you can save workspaces to your private account, and resume work on them when you log back in to your account from any computer. You can also click ‘Share’, which shares a workspace privately with another user, and ‘Publish’, which creates a permanent public version of your workspace that can be linked to from a research paper, or from elsewhere on the web.
          %p
            %strong Export and import.
            Use the ‘Export’ feature to download a copy of your workspace to your local machine, and ‘Import’ to upload a previously-download workspace. This functionality only involves your browser, not the globular.science server, so you do not need to log in or even create an account to use these features. If you import a workspace, log in, and click ‘Save’, then a copy of the imported workspace will be saved to the server.
          %p
            %strong Security and privacy.
            The plain text of your password is not stored on the server. However, your private workspaces are saved in plain text, and as such should not be considered highly secure, as they could be accessed by someone with site administrator credentials. The administrators pledge not to access any user data, except from the registered email address, without the permission of that individual user. In other words: your private work is private! User data is backed up nightly to a secure server.
          %h3#manipulating_diagrams Manipulating diagrams
          %p
            A diagram is a composite of the cells in the current signature, and basic diagram manipulation can be performed by clicking on the signature, as follows, where we suppose our diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is
            = succeed "-dimensional." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Creation.
              If the current diagram is empty, clicking the icon of a cell in the signature constructs a diagram which consists solely of the selected generator.
            %li
              %strong Rewriting.
              Selecting a
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              from the signature displays a list of all the ways that its source
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(G)
              can be identified as a subdiagram of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(G)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(G)
            %li
              %strong Attaching.
              Selecting a
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \leq k
              displays a list of all the ways that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              can be attached to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              along one of its boundaries. Choosing an element of this list performs the attachment.
          %p The menu on the right-hand side of the screen gives further commands. Each of these commands has a shortcut key, which is also given.
          %ul
            %li
              %strong
                Clear
                = succeed "." do
                  %em c
              Deletes the current diagram.
            %li
              %strong
                Identity
                = succeed "." do
                  %em i
              Builds the identity
              = succeed "-diagram" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              = succeed "-diagram." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %strong
                Source
                = succeed "." do
                  %em s
              Saves the current diagram as the source of a new generator.
            %li
              %strong
                Target
                = succeed "." do
                  %em t
              Saves the current diagram as the target of a new generator.
            %li
              %strong
                Restrict
                = succeed "." do
                  %em r
              Replaces the current diagram with the subdiagram currently being viewed (see
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#changingtheview"} Changing the view
            %li
              %strong
                Theorem
                = succeed "." do
                  %em h
              Creates a new theorem witnessing the current diagram (see
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "#invertibility"} Invertibility
            %li
              %strong
                Graphic
                = succeed "." do
                  %em g
              Downloads a PNG representation of the current diagram.
          %p Also, the user can click and click-and-drag on the diagram to produce various effects. When a command is ambiguous, a menu will be presented to allow the user to choose their preferred action.
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Click.
              This attempts to attach or rewrite, as appropriate for the location of the click, with an element of the signature. If an element of the signature is marked as invertible, then the higher structure this implies will also be considered for attachment.
            %li
              %strong Click-and-drag.
              This attempts to attach or rewrite using an interchanger, or naturality for the interchanger, or invertibility of some cell. This is sensitive to both the click location and the direction of the drag, in a way which should be intuitive. This also allows cells to be cancelled from the top or bottom of a diagram.
          %p
            Clicking and dragging is designed to work as if you were really ‘touching’ the strings. So if you want to braid one strand over another, click the strand to ‘grab’ it, and ‘pull’ it to one side. If you want to pull a vertex through a braiding, click the vertex to ‘grab’ it, and ‘pull’ it up or down through its adjacent braiding. Of course,
            %em Globular
            will only carry out the command if the move you are attempting to make is actually valid in that location.
          %p
            The most common move to perform is a basic interchanger, where two unrelated critical points with adjacent heights have their heights exchanged. Holding the Shift button while clicking-and-dragging tells
            %em Globular
            to only look for basic interchanger moves. This speeds up the processing, and is particularly useful for applying long chains of interchangers.
          %p In order to undo any change then click the back button in your browser. This feels very counter-intuitive but trust me it works!
          %h3#changingtheview Changing the view
          %p
            %em Globular
            can currently present string-diagram visualizations of 0-, 1- and 2-dimensional diagrams. Viewing a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \gt 2
            is made possible by
            %em projection
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em slicing
          %ul
            %li
              %strong Project.
              This determines the number of dimensions to be projected; it can take any value
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              in the range
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi p
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq p \leq k
              = succeed ";" do
                %em Globular
              %em singularity graphics
              which are a common tool in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse+theory"} higher Morse theory
            %li
              %strong Slice.
              Having decided to project out
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              dimensions, and supposing the image being displayed is
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                      %mo ∈
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \in \{0,1,2\}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mi p
                    %mo −
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k - p - v
              dimensions still to be handled. If this sum is positive, coordinates must be chosen using the slice controls to position the view within an appropriate
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi v
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
          %p You can also zoom the main diagram using the scroll wheel of your mouse, and scroll around by clicking-and-dragging with the right mouse button (on a Mac trackpad, this is a two-fingered press-hard-and-drag. Yes, this really is a thing. If it doesn’t seem to work, you’re not pressing hard enough—the trackpad should ‘click’ under your fingers.)
          %h3#technical_implementation_details Technical implementation details
          %p
            %i Globular
            is written in Javascript, and performs all its computations client-side, in the browser. Most of the computations it has to perform are linear in the size of the diagram, so this is quite computationally feasible, even for large proofs. The back-end is a Node.js server that allows the saving, sharing and publishing of workspaces. Graphics are implemented in SVG. Project data is compressed using the LZ4 algorithm.
          %h2#singularities Singularities
          %h3#overview_3 Overview
          %p
            In a 2-category, a 1-cell and a 2-cell can be composed in one direction (horizontal composition), and two 2-cell can be composed in two directions (horizontal and vertical composition.) In general, in a traditional globular
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtext min
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{min}(p,q)
            different directions: along a common boundary 0-cell, or 1-cell, and so on, up to a common boundary
            = succeed "-cell." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mtext min
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\text{min}(p,q)-1)
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %em Globular
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %em one
            way: along a common boundary
            = succeed "-cell." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mtext min
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\text{min}(p,q)-1)
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %em generic position
          %p
            For every way that
            %em Globular
            %em doesn’t
            let you compose cells—that is, for every
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q
            and every
            = succeed "\u2014there" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mtext min
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq n \lt \text{min}(p,q)
            = succeed "-cell," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mi q
                    %mo −
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p+q-n-1)
            %em resolves
            the singularity that would be created if this composite were actually formed. These singularity resolutions manifest in
            %em Globular
            as higher-dimensional cells, which allow parts of a diagram to move around each other; using them effectively is a key part of formalizing higher-dimensional proofs. The main difficulty in the definition of semistrict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            In this section we list the singularities which
            %em Globular
            recognizes. We organize them by categorical dimension, because to formalize a proof in an
            = succeed "-category," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq n
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cell." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-2)
          %h3#2category_singularities 2-category singularities
          %p
            %strong Interchangers
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              two
              = succeed "-cells" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %li
              %em Interface:
              When unprojected, click the vertex to be interchanged and drag it up or down; if the interchanger is ambiguous because the source or target is the identity, then make your drag slightly to the left or right. When projected, grab the wire to be interchanged, and drag in the desired direction.
          %p
            %img{:alt => "Interchanger source", :src => "http://globular.science/nlab/interchanger-source.png"}/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mpadded{:depth => "depth", :height => "+1.8cm", :voffset => "1.8cm"}
                    %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathraisebox{1.8cm}{\to}
            %img{:alt => "Interchanger target", :src => "http://globular.science/nlab/interchanger-target.png"}/
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \qquad\qquad
            %img{:alt => "Interchanger projection", :src => "http://globular.science/nlab/interchanger-projection.png"}/
          %h3#3category_singularities 3-category singularities
          %p
            %strong Naturality of the interchanger in one variable
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              a
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            %li
              %em Interface:
              …
          %h3#4category_singularities 4-category singularities
          %p
            %strong Naturality of naturality of the interchanger in one variable
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              a 2-cell and a 4-cell composed along a common boundary 0-cell, forming a 5-cell resolution.
            %li
              %em Interface:
              …
          %p
            %strong Naturality of the interchanger in two variables
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              two 3-cells composed along a common boundary 0-cell, forming a 5-cell resolution.
            %li
              %em Interface:
              …
          %p
            %strong Naturality of invertibility of the interchanger
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              …
            %li
              %em Interface:
              …
          %p
            %strong Contractible 3rd Reidemeisterators
          %ul
            %li
              %em Composite:
              …
            %li
              %em Interface:
              …
          %h2#other_features Other features
          %h3#invertibility Invertibility
          %p
            A
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A \to B
            in an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %em invertible
            when there is an inverse cell
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f ^{-1} : B \to A
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: \mathrm{id}_A \to f ^{-1} \circ f
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                      %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi f
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu: \mathrm{id}_B \to f \circ f ^{-1}
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %em Globular
          %h3#popup_labels Popup labels
          %p
            When you move your pointer over a diagram, a little label will pop up telling you what’s underneath the pointer. Doing this near a vertex, you will see that
            %i Globular
            isn’t really aware that the lines curve, so you will need to take care when clicking or clicking-and-dragging that
            %i Globular
            is correctly identifying the structure you are trying to interact with.
          %p
            The labels are written using the following syntax, where ‘X’ is some arbitrary label denoting a
            = succeed "-cell," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-cells." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %ul
            %li
              ‘X inverse’ describes the
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              ‘X, insert’ describes the canonical
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mo .
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \text{id} _{s(X)} \to X . X^{-1}
            %li
              ‘X, cancel’ describes the canonical
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo .
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mtext id
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X . X^{-1} \to \text{id} _{s(X)}
            %li ‘Interchanger’ describes the height-exchange cell
            %li ‘Pull-through interchanger above’, ‘Pull-through interchanger underneath’, ‘Pull-through inverse interchanger above’ and ‘Pull-through inverse interchanger underneath’ describe the 4 different ways that a cell can be pulled through an interchanger
          %p Note that interchangers are of dimension at least 3, and pull-throughs are of dimension at least 4.
          %h3#theorems Theorems
          %p The theorem command allows a diagram to be stored, in a such way that it can be reused as a component in subsequent diagrams. The idea is that the diagram is a ‘theorem’ proving that its source can be rewritten into its target. The ‘proof’ is the diagram itself.
          %p
            Concretely, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be the current diagram, which is
            = succeed "-dimensional," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(D)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(D)
            be its source and target
            = succeed "-diagrams." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k-1)
          %ul
            %li
              A
              = succeed "-generator" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              with source
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(D)
              and target
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t(D)
            %li
              An invertible
              = succeed "-generator" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k+1)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              with source
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              and with target
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %p
            The generator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            can be used in future proofs to ‘stand in’ for the diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            can be used to replace
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            with the content of diagram
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is invertible, it could also be used to identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            as a subdiagram of a larger proof, and replace it with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
          %h3#coordinates Coordinates
          %p
            Composites produced by
            %i Globular
            are in generic position; for example, no two vertices ever appear at the same height. As a result, there is a simple coordinate system that can be used to refer to elements of a diagram. For a
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo −
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-k+1)
            coordinates must be provided; that is, the number of coordinates is the codimension of the cell, plus 1. Moving the mouse cursor over a diagram in the screen shows the coordinate of the element underneath the cursor.
          %p
            The coordinate system for an
            = succeed "-diagram" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            has coordinate
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [y]
            = succeed "-cell" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \lt n
            has leading coordinate given by its height
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            in the diagram, and subsequent coordinates given by its coordinates in the slice diagram at that height.
